# F*, every layer a part reaches through the composition, itself included. One row per (layer,
# layer it reaches), and a layer always reaches itself.
#
# The third closure, and the tree already names the other two
#   `composition/descent.sqlc` is F+, the layers reachable in one step or more.
#   `rank/co_moving_layers.sqlc` is F+ meeting its own transpose, the layers that move together.
#   This is F* = F+ ∪ I, and it is the one an inclusion question needs: asking whether two parts
#   reach a common layer has to count a part that IS that layer, because naming a layer directly
#   while a sibling composes it is the commonest way for two parts to overlap.
#
# The identity arm is the parts, not the layer dimension
#   The reflexive half is the identity on the layers that appear as parts, taken from
#   `composition/parts.sqlc`. Taking it from `layers/every_layer.sqlc` would read the same on this
#   corpus and be a different relation: the dimension reaches everything by construction, so
#   composing it here would put every filed layer into the closure of every reader, and
#   `algebra/dimension_use.sqlc` is the law that refuses exactly that.
#
# UNION, not UNION ALL
#   A closure forgets how often. A layer reached by two routes is reached, and the question this
#   relation answers is whether, never how many times. Counting routes is `composition/descent`'s
#   job, where each route is still a row.
-- composition/descent.sqlc unioned with the identity on composition/parts.sqlc: F* = F+ ∪ I.
SELECT DISTINCT root_filing, root_layer, filing, layer
FROM (
    :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
) w
  UNION
SELECT DISTINCT part_filing, part_layer, part_filing, part_layer
FROM (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
